WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:09.679
I'm going to be using my iPad for notes.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.369
I promise I'm not on Yik Yak most of the time.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:16.554
So I'm a huge nerd for start-up history.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:20.339
I spent a fairly considerable fraction of my leisure time over the

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:26.239
past year reading histories of Oracle and Microsoft and Facebook.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.034
It was really exciting.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.789
But there is this phenomenon where if you're interested

00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:34.679
in start-ups or if you're thinking in start - if

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.534
you're thinking of starting a company,

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:39.459
you don't quite get the full picture,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:43.609
because companies want to put the best face forward.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.949
They want to whitewash things a little bit.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:50.909
And so, you would end - you don't end up seeing the really interesting

00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:53.949
detail and the uncertainty from the early days of a company.

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:58.859
And so what I'm going to try to do over the course of talking to you

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:03.099
today is give you a little bit of insight into that uncertainty,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.879
into what it's like in the early days.

00:01:06.170 --> 00:01:09.029
And if I don't do a good job with that,

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.049
in this part I will be going in to questions maybe half

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:15.444
an hour in and you can ask the tricky questions.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.239
So maybe we should start off with what is Stripe.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.569
And this is not maybe as easy a question as it sounds.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:31.289
So one answer is that Stripe is a really

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:33.209
easy way to accept payments online.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.429
This is why we started it back in 2009 when

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.389
we had been developing Web products,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.279
we had been developing apps.

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.909
The two options you really had if you wanted to turn your product into

00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:50.699
a business were - one, you could send your user's offsite to PayPal,

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.104
that experience kind of sucked.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.509
Or two, you could sign up for a merchant account and

00:01:55.510 --> 00:01:57.749
go through the weeks of work and building out your

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:00.139
own payments infrastructure that that entailed.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:01.479
Neither option is really good.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:06.309
And so, one way to look at Stripe is that,

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:10.249
a better way that came along to accept payments.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.569
But if you zoom out a little bit you can look at it

00:02:14.570 --> 00:02:17.699
as a platform for running your online business.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.219
So we've seen thanks to the Internet an

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:24.669
explosion of not just new businesses, but new kinds of businesses,

00:02:24.740 --> 00:02:28.739
new business models. You look at software that previously

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.539
was sold on a license basis is now delivered via

00:02:31.540 --> 00:02:33.589
the cloud as Software-as-a-Service.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:37.679
It's all subscription services or you see that services

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.119
that were previously kind of off-line delivered on an ad hoc

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:45.729
basis are being delivered by market places that can

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:49.819
help with discovery and liquidity and trust and safety.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.169
I'm talking about Airbnb for travel or Lyft and

00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.799
Uber in the transport segment and it goes,

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:01.549
into every vertical like home cleaning or errands.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.894
And so as all these new business models emerge,

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:10.284
to actually to run the business, if you are a subscription business,

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.539
to keep track of how much everyone is paying you and

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.659
actually bill them or if you're a marketplace,

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:18.089
to run the pretty complex operations and move all the money around in

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:22.879
a variety of different companies to make that happen this was hugely

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:25.299
challenging for people and they were all building it themselves.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:29.749
And so what Stripe really does is gives them a platform so that they

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:32.254
can use Stripe for all that and not have to build it themselves.

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.374
So maybe that's another definition.

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:37.449
But if you zoom out even further,

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:42.669
maybe Stripe is just better economic infrastructure for the Internet.

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:45.819
Thanks to the way the Internet has emerged.

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:50.844
We have this global system that lets anyone share information,

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:53.909
but money definitely doesn't work that way.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.809
In the U.S

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:57.179
we have credit cards and we think of them

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.844
as pretty global, but they are not.

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.139
In Kenya, everyone uses a mobile money system

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.614
called M-Pesa which is on feature phones,

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:08.189
it's really far advanced.

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:13.749
43% of Kenyan GDP flows through M-Pesa and no one really

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:18.279
uses a credit card or in China everyone uses Alipay,

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:20.504
the payment arm of Alibaba.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.379
And so we've erected this kind of Tower of Babel when

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.549
it comes to money on the Internet where we have a bunch

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:30.464
of different systems none of which interoperates.

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.619
An our idea with Stripe is that by using a Stripe you should just

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.364
not be able to have a system for processing credit cards,

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.699
but a way to accept money from anyone, anywhere.

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:46.069
And so, neither one of these answers is more correct than the other;

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.769
they are just all different answers at a variety of zoom levels.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.889
And one phenomenon I think is really interesting

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:59.339
about start-ups is they tend over time to get higher

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:02.209
and higher up in how they describe themselves.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.389
And that's okay, and that makes sense.

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:07.629
But the sneaky thing they do is they pretend that that's

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.709
how they viewed themselves all along and so you have this

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:14.659
great Silicon Valley phenomenon of the founding myth that

00:05:14.660 --> 00:05:17.039
we set out to revolutionize app discovery.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.509
I mean what does that even mean?

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:24.079
And one of my favorite examples of this

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:26.249
is I saw an interview with Elon Musk,

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:29.129
I mean Elon Musk is clearly an incredible entrepreneur

00:05:29.190 --> 00:05:30.889
and he's someone I respect a lot.

00:05:31.040 --> 00:05:33.519
But he was giving this interview and the

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:37.124
interviewer was asking him - payments,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:41.569
electric cars, space travel, what connects these,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:45.499
and Elon kind of looked very thoughtful and he was like well,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.429
when I was in college I thought the three biggest

00:05:48.430 --> 00:05:53.184
opportunities for mankind were the Internet,

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.779
inter-planetary space travel, and clean energy.

00:05:55.780 --> 00:05:58.074
I was like, really?

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.039
That visionary?

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:07.999
And so what that means is as you're trying to think,

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:14.449
is this a valid start-up idea, maybe you've identified a problem,

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:15.739
but it seems very tactical,

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:18.304
you don't know what the broader vision is.

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:22.569
One stark contrary example here is I really enjoyed

00:06:22.570 --> 00:06:27.129
Travis Kalanick in describing Uber recently,

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:31.259
Uber's latest tagline is transportation as reliable

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:34.309
as running water available to everyone.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:37.874
But Travis, I remember in a while ago described in

00:06:37.970 --> 00:06:41.269
an interview someone asked him why he started Uber?

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.059
And he said it was a lifestyle thing,

00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:45.189
me and my co-founder and our hundred friends could roll

00:06:45.190 --> 00:06:49.154
around San Francisco like ballers in Mercedes S classes.

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:53.199
So that's maybe a little before the transportation

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:56.249
as reliable as running water.

00:06:56.250 --> 00:06:59.909
So I guess the point I'm trying to make here is that you don't

00:06:59.910 --> 00:07:03.879
always know in the early days what the largest thing is.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:08.584
For me and Patrick, when we started Stripe,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:11.779
the market research we did was,

00:07:11.960 --> 00:07:14.994
we knew this was something we really wanted as developers

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:19.289
and we had one or two friends who wanted it and that

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.159
seems like an addressable market right there.

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:26.974
And over time we have come to realize that this is in fact

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:36.539
a really large and meaty problem that we can be tackling.

00:07:36.810 --> 00:07:41.219
The other challenge in the early days of start-ups,

00:07:41.220 --> 00:07:42.669
well that sounds kind of trite;

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.829
there are lot of challenges.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.684
Let's talk about another challenge.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:53.709
One of my favorite media tropes is the multi-year overnight success,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:57.509
people talking about how quickly start-ups get to scale.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:08:02.399
I mean even in Stripe's case I've seen lot of newspaper articles

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.504
that literally describe it as an overnight success.

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:08.459
It is like, hey you weren't there.

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:14.079
In our case we spent a really long time in the early

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:17.429
days building out the product and trying to figure

00:08:17.430 --> 00:08:21.334
out would it work and what the right thing was.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:26.429
And so to give you a sense of scale here we started working on Stripe

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:32.083
in the fall of 2009 and we launched Stripe in September 2011,

00:08:32.270 --> 00:08:34.504
which was roundabout two years later.

00:08:34.620 --> 00:08:38.409
And I remember right at the beginning when we were starting it,

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:42.019
I said to Patrick, yes, let's do it.

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:45.989
How hard can it be which gives you a sense for our mindset?

00:08:45.990 --> 00:08:50.949
And then, the answer was two years of difficulty.

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:53.929
We had not predicted that.

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.989
And when we launched two years later,

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:01.779
does anyone want to take a guess how many users Stripe had?

00:09:04.040 --> 00:09:05.969
Close, 50.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.389
So we had spent two years building out the

00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:13.019
early product and acquiring those 50 users.

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:19.399
And you know when you're spending two years getting 50 users;

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.329
it doesn't feel like a whole lot of progress,

00:09:21.330 --> 00:09:23.119
like it feels like things are going pretty slow.

00:09:23.310 --> 00:09:27.149
And this is the challenge if you have - if you're

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.984
working on a start-up that's a bad idea,

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.324
it's going to feel like slow going,

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:34.034
but also if you're working on a start-up that's a good idea,

00:09:34.130 --> 00:09:37.419
it may feel like slow going too.

00:09:37.660 --> 00:09:42.479
I think the thing that really allowed us to take off

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:46.219
in the subsequent years was the fact that since we were

00:09:46.220 --> 00:09:48.829
spending so much time on each one of those users,

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:51.599
since we were hyper focused on building out a great product

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:54.369
and since we weren't dealing with problems of scale yet,

00:09:54.370 --> 00:10:00.069
that allowed us to really build the product that we wanted.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:07.559
One of the cultures that set in really early on was taking

00:10:07.660 --> 00:10:12.729
almost abnormally good care of those early users.

00:10:12.840 --> 00:10:17.459
And so, I think someone wrote a blog post about this,

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.219
but we set up the Stripe API to e-mail

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.839
us any time someone ran into a bug.

00:10:24.950 --> 00:10:27.599
So when you run into bug on any site and

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.599
it shows the error message or something, in that case on Stripe,

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:33.489
it would e-mail us and occasionally phone call us.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.409
Because we figured when you're there as a developer integrating

00:10:37.410 --> 00:10:39.059
this product that you expect to be reliable,

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:40.239
running into that bug is a terrible

00:10:40.240 --> 00:10:42.184
experience and we wanted to fix it.

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:43.889
And so it would alert us,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:46.739
we would often, I mean we weren't doing anything else at the time

00:10:46.740 --> 00:10:50.339
we would often open our laptop and start fixing it and send the

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:54.529
user a friendly e-mail of hey that bug you discovered just there

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.169
it's now fixed and people's minds were blown by this.

00:10:57.170 --> 00:11:01.644
Sometimes they even found it a little bit creepy.

00:11:01.730 --> 00:11:07.229
We set up a Campfire room that any of our customers could come into,

00:11:07.370 --> 00:11:09.859
at really any hour of the day or night.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.574
We even - if people were based in the Bay Area,

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.779
we'd let them come by the office and we would just sit

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.599
over their shoulder and help them integrate Stripe.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:23.159
We had in the bottom right of the Stripe dashboard

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:26.559
a little prompt that would change on every page load.

00:11:26.560 --> 00:11:30.649
It would say, the thing you guys should improve here or

00:11:30.650 --> 00:11:33.714
a feature I really want is or the worst thing about that -

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:37.219
this page is and people would fill it out and hit enter.

00:11:37.300 --> 00:11:39.609
And again, because there were so few users,

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:43.789
we would reply within 10 minutes and people found it a little odd.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:47.429
What this meant was that even though the user growth

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.899
was happening quite slowly in the early days,

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:53.504
it actually had a really surprising viral affect

00:11:53.600 --> 00:11:56.099
where people had a really good experience,

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.679
they told their friends about it and we were able to spread

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.649
entirely through word of mouth even to this day.

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:08.099
We actually baked viral mechanics into the product and so for

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:11.914
the longest time it was available in invite only beta mode.

00:12:12.110 --> 00:12:15.979
And I guess you don't really think of payment

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:19.394
processing as a really viral social thing,

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:24.244
but the only way for the longest time to get access to Stripe was

00:12:24.380 --> 00:12:28.499
to get invited by someone who knew it and so again you had this

00:12:28.500 --> 00:12:31.549
phenomenon where people were not only signing up for Stripe,

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:35.739
but being recommended to it by someone who was already using it,

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:38.199
who knew how to use it and it really

00:12:38.200 --> 00:12:39.604
helped not just with user growth,

00:12:39.820 --> 00:12:43.339
but user engagement.

00:12:45.240 --> 00:12:50.204
The other challenge you'll face as you go through that

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:56.929
early-stage with those early customers is really figuring

00:12:56.930 --> 00:13:00.809
out what is core to the product and what is not.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:05.639
Maybe another way to phrase this is what is - what

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:07.779
should be part of your vision and what will need

00:13:07.780 --> 00:13:09.944
to change as you hit obstacles with customers,

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:13.679
you can even probably define vision as the

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.259
things that you're not willing to change.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:22.909
There tends to be a lot of debate over the lean start-up

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.399
methodology where you should have a product,

00:13:25.400 --> 00:13:28.939
show it to some customers rapidly iterate based on their usage

00:13:28.940 --> 00:13:34.824
and their feedback versus the very vision driven product of

00:13:34.980 --> 00:13:37.994
I know what this is and I know where it should go.

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:40.779
And to me that's not really a trade off at all

00:13:40.780 --> 00:13:43.574
in that you need some small set of things,

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:46.569
the vision that you're not really willing to compromise

00:13:46.570 --> 00:13:51.039
on and then with the remainder I mean if the market

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:52.264
is telling you that you're wrong,

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.109
you really need to listen to that data.

00:13:55.890 --> 00:14:01.729
In Stripe's case, I think we were really focused

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:03.969
on an instant on-boarding experience,

00:14:03.970 --> 00:14:09.209
building a developer friendly tool as our path to market and I

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:12.149
think that was stuff we were really willing to compromise on.

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:14.314
If that wasn't a valid way to build this company,

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:17.389
I'm not sure we would have been able to pivot out of that.

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:22.709
But in every other area we explored the market;

00:14:22.810 --> 00:14:25.659
we just kept running into feedback from customers that ended

00:14:25.660 --> 00:14:29.529
up being really critical to allowing the product to succeed.

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:33.754
So it's not a vision versus iteration trade-off.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:36.959
It's a selection of the things that you really believe in and then

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:42.039
being willing to contort yourself to make it work around that.

00:14:42.920 --> 00:14:47.859
And related to that in the vision category is that there

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:51.569
will be things in the early days of a start-up that makes

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:56.829
sense in the early days that don't scale later on and so

00:14:56.910 --> 00:15:00.929
it's not just a question of - it's more of a continuum,

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.119
there aren't just things that you should not change and things

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.789
like you should be willing to change on a regular basis.

00:15:06.870 --> 00:15:09.279
It's - there will be things that you should not change for the first

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.944
two years and then gradually need to be unwound as you grow.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:15.929
And so in the early days of a company,

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:18.459
it's probably really critical that they focus on the U.S

00:15:18.460 --> 00:15:21.334
market, trying to win all markets is a distraction,

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.999
but clearly over the long-term focusing solely on the U.S

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.309
market is wasteful and they should go international or Facebook for

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:33.074
the longest time they had the mantra of move fast and break things.

00:15:33.210 --> 00:15:34.849
And this really made sense for them,

00:15:34.850 --> 00:15:36.684
they were trying to have a really fast product cadence.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:41.619
But at a certain size you get so big and so interconnected that

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:43.849
moving fast and breaking things probably isn't your model and

00:15:43.850 --> 00:15:46.679
so they - I mean they recently explicitly acknowledged,

00:15:46.680 --> 00:15:48.969
that they were moving past this.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:54.289
Start-ups end up having a really powerful path dependence

00:15:54.480 --> 00:16:01.419
where it's not just a question of arriving at the final

00:16:01.420 --> 00:16:05.519
answer and if you had found that final answer earlier,

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:06.884
you could have gotten there much quicker,

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:09.479
it's a question of building a set of assumptions,

00:16:09.480 --> 00:16:11.574
following those and continually revisiting them.

00:16:11.660 --> 00:16:15.919
You're not just trying to arrive at the final form for your product;

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:18.589
you're trying to get all the intermediate forms

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:21.529
in the chemical reaction that gets you there.

00:16:21.530 --> 00:16:24.329
A good example of this is Microsoft.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:30.319
They started out in the hobbyist markets building basic compilers;

00:16:30.400 --> 00:16:32.139
they couldn't have stayed there,

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.369
and could never become the company they are

00:16:34.370 --> 00:16:38.904
today as a result of building compilers.

00:16:38.950 --> 00:16:40.779
They had to move up stream and they are

00:16:40.780 --> 00:16:42.399
now basically an enterprise company.

00:16:42.400 --> 00:16:43.689
But at the same time,

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:46.759
they couldn't have started as an enterprise company,

00:16:46.760 --> 00:16:48.439
because no one would have bought from them -

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:50.229
these like two random college kids.

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:53.339
And so again there's this path dependence to Microsoft's

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:56.344
growth where they had to become each one of their stages

00:16:56.420 --> 00:16:58.569
to allow them to jump to the next level.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:03.229
Maybe another example of this is going back to Uber.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:07.299
If they had just started out with the Uber X model today,

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.179
doing ride sharing, they probably would

00:17:09.180 --> 00:17:10.604
have been shut down pretty quickly.

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:15.399
It required them to - to succeed,

00:17:15.400 --> 00:17:19.399
they needed to start with the limo business,

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:22.229
get to such a scale that it was a well-known and well liked

00:17:22.230 --> 00:17:24.709
service and then they could start doing ride sharing in

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:30.089
some cities and prove that it was a popular model.

00:17:31.130 --> 00:17:34.699
The last thing I briefly wanted to touch on this

00:17:34.700 --> 00:17:41.829
topic is that of start-up opportunities.

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:44.429
People often ask this question of where

00:17:44.430 --> 00:17:46.134
do I find the idea for a start-up.

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:51.459
And I think in Stripe's case it was - in

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:54.084
hindsight it makes a lot of sense.

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:56.609
Building an online business was really,

00:17:56.610 --> 00:18:01.219
really hard pre-Stripe and starting a service to make it easier,

00:18:01.350 --> 00:18:02.939
would seem to make a lot of sense.

00:18:03.710 --> 00:18:08.149
And that goes for a lot of other companies as well.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:11.739
Anyone who had ever taken a cab in San Francisco should

00:18:11.740 --> 00:18:15.509
have had the idea for Uber or in the case of Slack;

00:18:15.510 --> 00:18:20.189
we internally were struggling with the problem of TeamChat.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:23.439
And not only that everyone we knew was struggling with

00:18:23.440 --> 00:18:27.649
the problem of TeamChat and so the idea to build a company

00:18:27.650 --> 00:18:32.189
to tackle that seems obvious in hindsight.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:37.389
But that obviousness in hindsight doesn't really help you, right?

00:18:37.390 --> 00:18:40.939
Because if you only realize these opportunities after they

00:18:40.940 --> 00:18:43.089
have come along and someone has shown you the path,

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.749
that doesn't really help you get anywhere.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:51.379
I don't know if any of you guys have read David

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:53.679
Foster Wallace's commencement speech,

00:18:53.680 --> 00:18:54.989
called This Is Water.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:58.159
It's really, really good and you should check it out,

00:18:58.160 --> 00:18:59.169
if you haven't read it.

00:18:59.350 --> 00:19:06.414
But in it, he uses the story of a big fish passing two little fish and

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:11.474
the big fish says to the little fish hey guys, how is the water?

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:14.119
And they swim along and pass and one turns to the

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:16.124
other and it's like what the hell is water?

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:23.389
And he used this anecdote as to then go on and explain about

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:26.689
being mindful in the day-to-day lives that we live.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.069
And they're sort of the little bit like that in as you

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:35.204
think about opportunities on a day-to-day basis.

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:41.244
We live in a world that's completely broken in lots and lots of

00:19:41.290 --> 00:19:46.359
small or big ways and we have come to just work around them,

00:19:46.360 --> 00:19:48.499
we have come to take them for granted,

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:54.399
and so to spot opportunities requires you to jump out of that

00:19:54.400 --> 00:19:59.009
mode briefly and question how things work and be unreasonable

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:02.014
in suggesting that it's not valid for them to work this way.

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:09.944
It should be interesting to note that the companies that are often successful

00:20:10.040 --> 00:20:15.094
in changing an industry are often not started by insiders,

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:19.459
because insiders know too much about that industry

00:20:19.460 --> 00:20:21.879
they've been swimming in that water for too long.

00:20:21.950 --> 00:20:26.879
Certainly it was the case in Stripe's case as we came

00:20:26.880 --> 00:20:30.449
to learn more about how the payments industry worked.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:35.639
We could see the reasons why everything worked the way it did.

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:38.159
Before Stripe to get set up with payments online,

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:42.409
generally took a few weeks and it was a whole bunch of paper work.

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:46.649
As you get more into the banking industry and people say,

00:20:46.730 --> 00:20:50.539
well, we need to collect that information to run our underwriting

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:53.099
checks and fulfill our KYC BSA requirements,

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:54.329
you are like yes, yes.

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:57.039
And it requires you to stand back and say, no, no wait a second.

00:20:57.040 --> 00:20:58.749
This is not how the Internet works.

00:20:58.910 --> 00:21:05.879
And so I think there is a certain practice you get - you

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:09.119
have to get into in how you look at the world to stop

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:12.709
accepting everything is reasonable in how it works.

00:21:12.790 --> 00:21:16.129
We are all swimming in opportunity, but it's often really,

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:17.279
really hard to see it.

00:21:18.140 --> 00:21:19.769
And with that, I think we might take

00:21:19.770 --> 00:21:22.019
a break there and move to questions.

00:21:22.020 --> 00:21:22.364
Great.

00:21:22.480 --> 00:21:23.249
Perfect.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:26.534
So, this way it's going to work.

00:21:26.620 --> 00:21:28.429
I'm going to ask the first couple of questions,

00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:29.829
but I'm going to open it up to you,

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:32.329
so you can be noodling on the type of

00:21:32.330 --> 00:21:33.929
questions that you want to ask John.

00:21:34.420 --> 00:21:38.449
You mentioned several times starting this with your brother.

00:21:38.450 --> 00:21:41.209
Is that a good idea?

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:44.069
Tell us about, I mean, obviously it's been very successful,

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.959
but tell us about what it's like and what the opportunities and

00:21:47.960 --> 00:21:52.989
the challenges of starting a business with a family member?

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:53.199
Yes.

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:57.839
So I get this question all the time often from reporters or whatever.

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:01.519
And generally my response is to say that yes,

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.109
we got all our disagreements out of the way when we were

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:05.769
five and then try to move the conversation along.

00:22:05.920 --> 00:22:11.089
But now that I've told you that, so that wouldn't work here.

00:22:11.320 --> 00:22:17.854
I think starting a company with someone is,

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:20.019
I mean, one just on the face of it,

00:22:20.020 --> 00:22:26.699
it's hard, it's often seven hours - seven-day weeks and 14 hour days

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:31.879
again and again and again and going through a lot of uncertainty,

00:22:31.880 --> 00:22:35.929
a lot of setbacks, but also in the actual

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:37.839
work you're doing and the working style,

00:22:37.930 --> 00:22:42.899
you're probably the group of people starting the company

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:46.389
are learning a huge amount as they go along,

00:22:46.390 --> 00:22:49.779
that they are wrong a lot and discovering they're wrong a lot,

00:22:49.890 --> 00:22:52.709
and all this adds up to you need to have

00:22:52.710 --> 00:22:58.389
a really good working connection, if you already know the person,

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:00.449
you need to have a really good working connection

00:23:00.450 --> 00:23:02.809
with them and if you don't know the person,

00:23:02.810 --> 00:23:04.574
you need to develop that really fast.

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:07.749
And so I think we were lucky in that, Patrick and I, I mean,

00:23:07.750 --> 00:23:09.149
not only had we grown up together,

00:23:09.150 --> 00:23:10.549
but we had worked on a startup before,

00:23:10.550 --> 00:23:11.939
we had worked on projects before,

00:23:12.010 --> 00:23:15.749
and so all the meta issues of working style and how we worked together

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:19.319
were out of the way and we could focus squarely on the task in hand.

00:23:19.320 --> 00:23:24.849
And I think if you look at a lot of successful teams,

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:30.189
generally they had a history of working together,

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:31.919
you often see people who have worked together for a long

00:23:31.920 --> 00:23:34.184
time at previous jobs starting a company together.

00:23:34.330 --> 00:23:37.159
I'm not saying that's a necessary requirement,

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:40.029
but I think if you actually look at the data you have this phenomenon

00:23:40.030 --> 00:23:44.769
where it requires you to either get lucky in finding someone that you

00:23:44.770 --> 00:23:48.509
work really well with or pick someone where know that's the case.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:50.719
Did you have a very clear division of labor from the

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:52.579
beginning or is that something that's evolved?

00:23:52.680 --> 00:23:55.734
It was totally unclear in the beginning in that there was

00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:59.119
this huge amount of work and we both just tackled it.

00:23:59.500 --> 00:23:59.859
Great.

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.759
And you chose to go to Y Combinator.

00:24:01.760 --> 00:24:04.389
Can you tell us a little bit about that experience

00:24:04.390 --> 00:24:07.284
and how it influenced the evolution of the company?

00:24:07.610 --> 00:24:08.044
Yes.

00:24:08.160 --> 00:24:15.419
I think YC is really valuable,

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:22.939
especially for people like us where we were fairly new to

00:24:22.940 --> 00:24:28.689
the Valley and we were working in a business that required

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:34.254
us to understand how the Valley operated and worked.

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:38.069
It would have been very useful to have a network for hiring,

00:24:38.070 --> 00:24:39.439
for our business development,

00:24:39.440 --> 00:24:45.439
for raising money things like this and we didn't and most investors

00:24:45.440 --> 00:24:49.959
will say on their homepage that they don't accept cold pitches.

00:24:50.030 --> 00:24:52.329
You have to be in that network already to

00:24:52.330 --> 00:24:54.679
be able to participate in this system.

00:24:54.810 --> 00:24:57.719
And so YC is quite cool and unusual in the way that there

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:01.249
is a standard application form on the site and that's actually

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:05.429
the only way that they accept pitches is you - anyone from anywhere

00:25:05.430 --> 00:25:08.334
in the world can fill that out and get a spot.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:12.319
And so, I think what was valuable for us was

00:25:12.320 --> 00:25:15.469
the fact that we were pretty new to this;

00:25:15.470 --> 00:25:18.349
it helped us get into this world.

00:25:18.450 --> 00:25:21.619
And you know to give you a concrete example,

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:23.349
in the early days of Stripe,

00:25:23.350 --> 00:25:28.249
it was I mean, it was a lot of work convincing people to use it.

00:25:28.250 --> 00:25:32.099
It was this new unproven tool that you're

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:34.209
going to build your whole business on.

00:25:34.210 --> 00:25:36.334
I mean, that's kind of a scary undertaking.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:40.829
And so, it was - a lot of our early customers

00:25:40.830 --> 00:25:43.899
were YC companies who we knew through that,

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:48.419
they knew we weren't going to disappear in the morning

00:25:48.420 --> 00:25:51.199
and they were willing to integrate Stripe.

00:25:51.580 --> 00:25:52.229
Fabulous.

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:55.794
So anyone in the room want to start out with the first question?

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:56.409
Yes.

00:25:56.410 --> 00:25:57.049
Okay.

00:25:58.540 --> 00:26:02.234
What I want to know is your childhood related with entrepreneur.

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:02.869
Childhood?

00:26:03.090 --> 00:26:03.804
Childhood.

00:26:03.990 --> 00:26:10.434
Yes your days of childhood related with entrepreneur.

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:13.879
Was the question asking about his childhood as an entrepreneur?

00:26:13.880 --> 00:26:14.499
Yes.

00:26:15.050 --> 00:26:18.009
Childhood as an entrepreneur.

00:26:18.010 --> 00:26:18.499
Okay.

00:26:19.600 --> 00:26:19.739
Okay.

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:23.129
Grew up in the middle of nowhere in Ireland in a small

00:26:23.130 --> 00:26:29.869
town in County Tipperary and read a lot of books,

00:26:29.870 --> 00:26:31.989
used the internet a lot,

00:26:32.150 --> 00:26:38.309
I mean, childhood pre-Internet and post-Internet do seem to

00:26:38.310 --> 00:26:43.559
be very different in that pre-Internet you're mostly limited

00:26:43.560 --> 00:26:47.499
to a very local circle and post-Internet you're making friends

00:26:47.500 --> 00:26:50.249
with people all over the world and learning new things.

00:26:50.360 --> 00:26:52.729
I first coded when I was 14.

00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:58.489
I built a website that Patrick subsequently - Patrick is my brother,

00:26:58.490 --> 00:26:59.949
subsequently comprehensively hacked,

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:04.484
which I guess was a good lesson in never try anything.

00:27:04.610 --> 00:27:12.039
I'm not still bitter about that one.

00:27:12.410 --> 00:27:17.239
But yes, I think 20 years ago,

00:27:17.390 --> 00:27:23.719
would it have been as easy for me to come out here from - to come out

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:28.329
here from there and do something like Stripe, it seems unlikely.

00:27:29.650 --> 00:27:32.749
So clearly you are someone who has a lot of ideas, right?

00:27:32.750 --> 00:27:34.914
Starting at a very young age, you were building things,

00:27:35.010 --> 00:27:39.849
how did you pick this as the thing that you set I know I'm

00:27:39.850 --> 00:27:44.454
going to put all my energy behind this problem to solve?

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.619
Because this was a huge problem for a lot of us, right.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:49.459
It can be an interesting dinner table conversation or hanging

00:27:49.460 --> 00:27:51.069
out with the friends saying, this is a problem;

00:27:51.070 --> 00:27:52.269
someone should solve this,

00:27:52.390 --> 00:27:54.859
which is the one you say this one I'm going to solve?

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:55.529
Yes.

00:27:55.530 --> 00:27:58.909
I think that's a really hard problem.

00:27:58.910 --> 00:28:04.619
It sort of comes back to the bad ideas take a long

00:28:04.620 --> 00:28:06.859
time and never quite achieve escape velocity,

00:28:06.920 --> 00:28:09.709
but good ideas often take a really long

00:28:09.710 --> 00:28:12.159
time to achieve escape velocity too.

00:28:12.450 --> 00:28:15.589
In the case of Stripe,

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:19.479
it was actually - for the first few months we were

00:28:19.480 --> 00:28:21.369
also working on the series of iPhone apps,

00:28:21.370 --> 00:28:23.809
it was one of the projects we were working on,

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:25.729
but we were also in college at the time so we were juggling

00:28:25.730 --> 00:28:27.539
kind of class and these iPhone apps and Stripe,

00:28:27.660 --> 00:28:30.174
and so it was definitely not our exclusive focus.

00:28:30.370 --> 00:28:35.639
I think the thing that really convinced us in the early

00:28:35.640 --> 00:28:40.299
days was and this might be specific to more developer

00:28:40.300 --> 00:28:42.579
or business focused companies rather than consumer,

00:28:42.580 --> 00:28:44.404
which can get to scale really quickly,

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:51.319
but I think in the - in our case what really convinced us

00:28:51.430 --> 00:28:56.399
was the fact that the users we had were so passionate.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:59.864
This was not just for them, something marginally better,

00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:02.439
but they thought that it was life changing and revolutionary

00:29:02.440 --> 00:29:04.279
and they wanted to tell all their friends and they wanted us

00:29:04.280 --> 00:29:07.449
to build a way for them to invite their friends to it,

00:29:07.450 --> 00:29:12.269
that seemed to really get - that got our attention.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:14.879
And so I guess, most of the metrics that people talk

00:29:14.880 --> 00:29:17.139
about day-to-day like revenue or user numbers,

00:29:17.220 --> 00:29:19.579
those are all lagging metrics and you as the

00:29:19.580 --> 00:29:20.879
person actually working on the product,

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:22.589
you have access to leading metrics.

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:28.649
And so apparently with Snapchat,

00:29:28.650 --> 00:29:31.819
one of the earliest - one of the most compelling metrics in

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:37.479
the early days was the number of times per day each person

00:29:37.480 --> 00:29:40.039
used it in that even if this wasn't really big yet,

00:29:40.040 --> 00:29:42.399
it was growing very quickly and it seemed to have a really

00:29:42.400 --> 00:29:45.929
deep connection with each of its users and similarly

00:29:45.930 --> 00:29:48.999
for us I think the passion that the early users had about

00:29:49.000 --> 00:29:51.399
the product was that leading metric that,

00:29:51.400 --> 00:29:55.839
that indicated to us that something was there.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:29:58.679
But I think in each case that leading

00:29:58.680 --> 00:29:59.799
metric is going to be different,

00:29:59.800 --> 00:30:01.899
but you want to find what that leading metric is to be

00:30:01.900 --> 00:30:04.224
able to judge whether this is going to take off or not.

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:07.259
So this is a space that has become very,

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:09.609
very busy with lots of other companies,

00:30:09.610 --> 00:30:11.859
how do you think about your competition?

00:30:11.860 --> 00:30:14.959
Is this something that you think of it is a cat and mouse game,

00:30:14.960 --> 00:30:17.669
with features, do you say I don't pay attention to them;

00:30:17.670 --> 00:30:18.959
we have got our own path?

00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:20.804
Talk a little bit about competitors.

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:28.479
Yes, it's sort of funny in the early days when we started Stripe,

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:30.619
people would say to us like really, payments?

00:30:30.620 --> 00:30:33.839
Why would you start a payments company and then in 2012 there were

00:30:33.840 --> 00:30:36.614
like all these payments companies and people would say to us,

00:30:36.850 --> 00:30:39.069
man payments, that's like a super hot space,

00:30:39.070 --> 00:30:41.489
that was a great idea you guys starting that and you're just like -

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:45.659
the whole time we were like nonplussed by all these reactions.

00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:54.279
I think there's - it's important to be really

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:56.984
clear in your own mind and as time goes on,

00:30:57.100 --> 00:31:01.849
be good at communicating what is unique about what you're doing.

00:31:01.850 --> 00:31:03.264
And so in the early days,

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:06.574
people would always ask us they were like so,

00:31:06.880 --> 00:31:09.859
payments and like with a design focus and it starts with S,

00:31:09.860 --> 00:31:11.809
so it's like sort of like square, right?

00:31:11.810 --> 00:31:14.619
And actually when you think about it,

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:16.584
they are two entirely different businesses

00:31:16.650 --> 00:31:21.149
in that Stripe is a technology product,

00:31:21.150 --> 00:31:23.344
using it more or less always involves code,

00:31:23.410 --> 00:31:26.809
we are interested in what you can build using this toolkit

00:31:26.810 --> 00:31:29.569
we've developed for you in a really leveraged way online,

00:31:29.570 --> 00:31:32.009
whereas Square is entirely offline,

00:31:32.010 --> 00:31:34.249
almost entirely aimed at the small business

00:31:34.250 --> 00:31:36.874
segment and offer face-to-face transactions.

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:41.439
And I think similarly with lots of competitors,

00:31:41.440 --> 00:31:44.784
people who only have a surface level understanding of your business,

00:31:44.920 --> 00:31:50.019
will tend to want to lump you into some category and it's very

00:31:50.020 --> 00:31:55.599
important that you be able to get across what it is that's not

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:58.234
just happens to be different today in the feature set,

00:31:58.340 --> 00:32:00.999
but what's different about where you're going and in the

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.954
asymptotes of these two companies where they end up.

00:32:04.150 --> 00:32:08.539
And I think that's not only important to be able to explain it,

00:32:08.540 --> 00:32:11.314
but it's important in an existential sense in that

00:32:11.380 --> 00:32:13.409
hopefully you're doing something different.

00:32:13.660 --> 00:32:15.139
So speaking of doing something different,

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:17.939
I understand you now deal in Bitcoin as well.

00:32:17.940 --> 00:32:21.379
That's obviously something that's very interesting

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:22.689
and a little bit controversial.

00:32:22.690 --> 00:32:25.709
Maybe can talk about what you see is the future

00:32:25.710 --> 00:32:27.729
of alternative currencies like Bitcoin?

00:32:27.820 --> 00:32:28.364
Yep.

00:32:28.670 --> 00:32:41.159
So I mentioned earlier the Tower of Babel effect

00:32:41.200 --> 00:32:43.909
where online we have all these different payment

00:32:43.910 --> 00:32:48.499
systems that are completely non-interoperable.

00:32:48.500 --> 00:32:51.249
And so we in the West happily use our credit cards,

00:32:51.250 --> 00:32:53.509
people in Africa use a variety of mobile money systems,

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:55.569
same in various parts of Asia,

00:32:55.680 --> 00:32:58.239
people in China use their Alipay accounts,

00:32:58.350 --> 00:33:03.919
you have this perfectly connected online world and

00:33:03.920 --> 00:33:07.209
then this perfectly balkanized economic landscape.

00:33:07.330 --> 00:33:12.119
And it's really harmful because it means then the products

00:33:12.120 --> 00:33:16.519
we use are artificially limited by you have the U.S

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:20.009
Internet, and you have the Chinese Internet as a result.

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:24.889
And so I think Bitcoin is - what is most exciting

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:28.259
for me about Bitcoin is that global aspect.

00:33:28.260 --> 00:33:30.469
It's the fact that everyone's Bitcoins are the same,

00:33:30.470 --> 00:33:35.699
everyone's Bitcoins work together with each other and that no one

00:33:35.700 --> 00:33:39.119
has to come along and enable the coin for a certain currency,

00:33:39.120 --> 00:33:41.929
there's new committee that decides that Bitcoin

00:33:41.930 --> 00:33:43.509
is going to South Africa in 2018.

00:33:43.510 --> 00:33:45.579
It's just there, that's how it works.

00:33:45.770 --> 00:33:52.679
And so, we recently added Bitcoin to Stripe and

00:33:52.680 --> 00:33:55.189
it's one of the instruments you can accept.

00:33:55.310 --> 00:33:57.194
And from a product point of view,

00:33:57.300 --> 00:33:58.649
one of the things that we have always thought

00:33:58.650 --> 00:34:01.334
is really important is moving beyond this,

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:04.459
I render a credit card form on my site that works for a very small

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:08.919
set of people to being that universal connector for people that you

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:12.044
just say I want to accept money and Stripe let's you accept money from

00:34:12.070 --> 00:34:14.619
anyone no matter what they're doing be it Bitcoin or whatever.

00:34:14.620 --> 00:34:18.214
The other thing I will say about Bitcoin is I think with

00:34:18.310 --> 00:34:23.149
that original Bitcoin paper that is phenomenally prescient

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:27.699
in retrospect like it is a truly great standalone paper

00:34:27.700 --> 00:34:30.519
that appeared out of nowhere and still people aren't quite

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:35.469
sure who even wrote it and what that's done is: one,

00:34:35.470 --> 00:34:38.469
it's kicked off a Bitcoin movement,

00:34:38.570 --> 00:34:42.459
but two it's kicked off a whole crypto currency movement that has

00:34:42.460 --> 00:34:45.469
got people thinking about this space in a way they weren't before

00:34:45.550 --> 00:34:50.679
and so people are arguing about what is the perfect consensus algorithm

00:34:50.680 --> 00:34:54.829
and what are the right constants in the hashing functions,

00:34:54.830 --> 00:34:59.169
if you are going to use hashing or proof of work or whatever and you

00:34:59.170 --> 00:35:03.149
have people arguing about every detail and you have some people,

00:35:03.490 --> 00:35:07.569
bemoaning the fracturing that's happening where you have all these

00:35:07.570 --> 00:35:10.564
altcoins like Litecoin and Zerocoin and things like this.

00:35:10.720 --> 00:35:15.949
But to me that's really exciting in that people are not only saying,

00:35:16.070 --> 00:35:17.499
this is a really cool technology,

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:20.009
but this is a really cool technology space and we should

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:22.299
explore all the possibilities that are in it.

00:35:22.300 --> 00:35:24.814
Stripe funded a nonprofit called Stellar,

00:35:24.860 --> 00:35:29.179
which is developing a crypto currency,

00:35:29.260 --> 00:35:35.109
it's very interesting it lets you trade not just with Bitcoin,

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:37.109
the currency is Bitcoin whereas with Stellar you

00:35:37.110 --> 00:35:39.759
can actually trade dollars or euros or pounds,

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:42.719
things denominated in the actual currency and it doesn't

00:35:42.720 --> 00:35:47.299
have - it doesn't use hashing for consensus or proof

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:53.169
of work it uses kind of a more CA like model.

00:35:53.280 --> 00:35:57.939
And so I think crypto currencies are totally in

00:35:57.940 --> 00:36:00.389
line with this general mission we believe in,

00:36:00.390 --> 00:36:04.189
which is the Internet economy should not be the U.S.,

00:36:04.190 --> 00:36:05.869
it should be the world and we need to

00:36:05.870 --> 00:36:07.544
fight really hard to make that happen.

00:36:07.780 --> 00:36:08.504
Terrific.

00:36:08.790 --> 00:36:09.994
Who has a question?

00:36:10.310 --> 00:36:10.779
Yes.

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:15.164
So you spoke at how to start a start-up course last quarter and that

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:19.074
went into everything that's relevant to startups to a great extent.

00:36:19.170 --> 00:36:21.869
We have this amazing amount of information now available

00:36:21.870 --> 00:36:24.349
in the Valley about what we should do.

00:36:24.640 --> 00:36:26.609
But it seems like some of the best founders out there,

00:36:26.610 --> 00:36:27.889
the ones who have been most successful

00:36:27.890 --> 00:36:29.739
didn't have this information at all.

00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:34.349
So how do you attribute this ability to find out what's

00:36:34.350 --> 00:36:36.459
the right thing to do in terms of culture and hiring

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:38.044
when no one has really told you about it?

00:36:38.210 --> 00:36:39.319
So I'm going to repeat the question.

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:40.834
I'm going to see if I've got it,

00:36:40.920 --> 00:36:43.419
is that listen, there is all this how to

00:36:43.420 --> 00:36:45.774
start a company information out there,

00:36:45.900 --> 00:36:47.959
you didn't have access to it.

00:36:47.960 --> 00:36:50.599
What's the difference between those people who read all the books,

00:36:50.600 --> 00:36:51.859
watch all the videos,

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:54.399
are listening right now and the people like

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:58.189
you who did it by the seat of your pants.

00:36:58.460 --> 00:37:05.684
Well, I think it's maybe giving us a little bit

00:37:05.930 --> 00:37:13.339
too much credit to say that we succeeded entirely

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:18.059
of our own accord and had no one helping us.

00:37:18.060 --> 00:37:21.689
There is the old quip about libertarians being

00:37:21.690 --> 00:37:24.749
mostly rich people and believing in the power of

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:29.079
the free market to get them to where they are.

00:37:29.080 --> 00:37:35.969
But I think in our case we were very lucky to have

00:37:36.100 --> 00:37:39.589
a lot of advice from people who had done it before,

00:37:39.590 --> 00:37:42.204
be it people via the YC network,

00:37:42.340 --> 00:37:45.974
Peter Thiel was a very early investor in Stripe.

00:37:46.180 --> 00:37:47.879
That was a fun one.

00:37:47.880 --> 00:37:50.374
He was actually our first significant investor,

00:37:50.520 --> 00:37:53.169
going into the office of Peter Thiel and saying

00:37:53.400 --> 00:37:55.324
online payments are totally broken,

00:37:55.570 --> 00:37:58.759
who caused this mess.

00:37:58.760 --> 00:38:03.919
But he knows an awful lot about building companies,

00:38:03.920 --> 00:38:06.159
not just through PayPal,

00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:08.689
but being the first outside investor in Facebook,

00:38:08.690 --> 00:38:11.789
a co-founder of Palantir and these other

00:38:11.790 --> 00:38:14.749
successful companies same with Sequioa,

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:18.949
things like this or studying what various

00:38:18.950 --> 00:38:23.089
companies that emerged in the '70s,

00:38:23.090 --> 00:38:27.729
'80s, '90s did and trying to draw out the conclusions

00:38:27.730 --> 00:38:29.499
of what made them successful like I said,

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.319
if you - if any of you guys want to join

00:38:31.320 --> 00:38:33.679
my fun startup history reading club,

00:38:33.680 --> 00:38:34.609
you're welcome too.

00:38:34.610 --> 00:38:39.149
But I don't think we were on our own at all.

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:42.469
There has always being a pretty good source of advice and going

00:38:42.470 --> 00:38:48.079
back to the point of whitewashing and the corporate history is

00:38:48.080 --> 00:38:50.239
always tending to be a bit more of an official history.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:52.789
For me the best advice has always been

00:38:52.790 --> 00:38:54.549
someone who was there first hand,

00:38:54.640 --> 00:38:57.629
who in a one on one context is willing to tell

00:38:57.630 --> 00:39:00.909
you not only the official sanitized version,

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:03.349
but and here is the time we nearly lost everyone's data,

00:39:03.510 --> 00:39:06.929
which actually happened for a major consumer Internet company.

00:39:07.630 --> 00:39:08.229
Great.

00:39:08.570 --> 00:39:09.079
Yes?

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:12.129
What worries you most about Stripe and what challenges

00:39:12.480 --> 00:39:17.359
is the company trying to overcome today?

00:39:17.360 --> 00:39:19.149
So the question is what worries you to the most right

00:39:19.150 --> 00:39:25.509
now and what challenges are you trying to overcome?

00:39:27.930 --> 00:39:32.329
Probably two things and I think the things we worry

00:39:32.330 --> 00:39:35.189
about and the challenges are sort of the same,

00:39:35.190 --> 00:39:39.749
so I will answer them together.

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:47.509
I think one is that product strategy, product prioritization,

00:39:47.510 --> 00:39:50.149
product planning, whatever you want to call it,

00:39:50.270 --> 00:39:52.459
is always going to be really hard.

00:39:52.460 --> 00:39:56.969
And I think people think it's going to

00:39:56.970 --> 00:39:59.149
be hard if things aren't going well,

00:39:59.150 --> 00:40:01.524
but it's even hard if things are going well,

00:40:01.680 --> 00:40:07.209
because you're trying to model the future and see the knock

00:40:07.210 --> 00:40:11.554
on effects of doing things with huge amounts of uncertainty.

00:40:11.700 --> 00:40:19.689
And so in our case we have a very successful subscription

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:22.919
product that we really believe in and we're expanding

00:40:23.040 --> 00:40:26.119
and we're also expanding internationally.

00:40:26.120 --> 00:40:29.279
And there is only a finite amount that we can work on and so,

00:40:29.350 --> 00:40:32.629
you're at a certain point faced with a choice,

00:40:32.630 --> 00:40:37.379
should we be investing in subscriptions or expanding internationally?

00:40:37.380 --> 00:40:40.259
The answer is both and you have to always be making these

00:40:40.260 --> 00:40:44.239
calls and constantly revisiting your calls when it comes

00:40:44.240 --> 00:40:47.549
to what you should be working on and it's so,

00:40:47.550 --> 00:40:50.669
so hard to predict up front what actually ends

00:40:50.670 --> 00:40:54.909
up being kind of a major part of your business.

00:40:55.140 --> 00:40:56.839
So I just want to build on this,

00:40:56.840 --> 00:40:59.264
how often do you revisit your strategy?

00:40:59.430 --> 00:41:01.269
Because you can't do it every day,

00:41:01.270 --> 00:41:03.849
it's sort of like digging up the seed everyday

00:41:03.850 --> 00:41:05.734
to see if it's sprouted, okay?

00:41:05.860 --> 00:41:08.599
But you also can't do it until it's an oak and you go oh my gosh,

00:41:08.600 --> 00:41:09.709
I really wanted a cherry.

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:10.369
Yes.

00:41:10.730 --> 00:41:17.929
I think the strategy you revisit all the time,

00:41:18.040 --> 00:41:22.179
I think what companies have to get good at is being willing

00:41:22.180 --> 00:41:27.879
to let things take the time to - being willing to give

00:41:27.880 --> 00:41:31.029
them a chance to actually go somewhere or not,

00:41:31.030 --> 00:41:35.064
because especially as the company grows you have

00:41:35.190 --> 00:41:36.929
all this pressure and not just externally,

00:41:36.930 --> 00:41:39.464
but the pressure you all set on yourselves internally

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:42.319
and one of the tougher things is saying,

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:46.679
look I know that the needle hasn't moved in on this yet,

00:41:46.820 --> 00:41:50.609
but we said initially that we believe this is going

00:41:50.610 --> 00:41:52.969
to be really important going forward and we still

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:57.909
believe that and it's just going to take time.

00:41:57.910 --> 00:42:00.874
I think - say Bitcoin is a perfect example.

00:42:00.980 --> 00:42:05.379
I think if you look at - if you go ask anyone who is accepting Bitcoin

00:42:05.380 --> 00:42:09.169
is it a significant fraction of their transaction volume,

00:42:09.170 --> 00:42:09.919
the answer will be no,

00:42:09.920 --> 00:42:12.839
because the consumer demand isn't there yet and so you're

00:42:12.840 --> 00:42:15.749
in this chicken and egg situation and so is it irrational

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:18.214
for these people to have Bitcoin on their sites?

00:42:18.310 --> 00:42:18.829
I mean, no.

00:42:18.830 --> 00:42:21.299
It's just that they want to give it that time and

00:42:21.300 --> 00:42:23.879
so they are probably constantly revisiting their

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:27.664
strategy of should we be investing in Bitcoin,

00:42:27.850 --> 00:42:32.494
but the secret sauce is in the patience to know when

00:42:32.620 --> 00:42:35.309
you should be giving things time and that's an art,

00:42:35.310 --> 00:42:36.149
that's like really an art.

00:42:36.150 --> 00:42:38.209
So you can be patient when it's your

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:39.669
company and there aren't investors,

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:41.449
but you now have a lot of investors

00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:43.519
who probably have a strong opinion.

00:42:43.520 --> 00:42:46.009
So you've got a board and you meet with them,

00:42:46.010 --> 00:42:49.219
can you tell us a little bit about your relationship with

00:42:49.220 --> 00:42:52.999
your board and how - maybe how you manage the board,

00:42:53.000 --> 00:42:54.389
how the board manages you?

00:42:54.390 --> 00:42:58.189
You've got to find patient investors.

00:42:58.320 --> 00:43:08.744
I think the good investors get that this is how this works.

00:43:08.890 --> 00:43:13.549
And they - the good investors have also seen it some amount

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:22.973
before and so in Facebook's case does anyone remember that

00:43:23.073 --> 00:43:25.749
Facebook in the very early days used to have banner ads?

00:43:26.463 --> 00:43:28.589
They were just going to sell ad space on Facebook that

00:43:28.590 --> 00:43:30.549
you could buy the banner at the top of the page.

00:43:30.680 --> 00:43:33.759
But it took them a few iterations before they found

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:36.739
a really good revenue model that's now doing well.

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:43.849
And so I think again in that collective amnesia that we all have where

00:43:43.850 --> 00:43:50.099
there's - we only really remember the successful stuff and it's easy

00:43:50.100 --> 00:43:53.799
to view it as why can't you just be like Facebook and quickly arrive

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:58.259
at the correct answer and it's important that the investors you find

00:43:58.260 --> 00:44:01.379
be able to understand that part of the creative process.

00:44:01.520 --> 00:44:02.209
Great.

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:03.459
Question, yes.

00:44:03.730 --> 00:44:06.739
Can you speak to the personal transition you've

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:09.529
had going from when you were just an engineer or

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:11.994
working in the early days to now being CEO,

00:44:12.030 --> 00:44:15.299
like do you enjoy being CEO or I don't actually know you're the CEO,

00:44:15.300 --> 00:44:19.569
but like cofounder like do you enjoy that role more?

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:20.469
Do you miss coding?

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:22.069
Do you still code sometimes?

00:44:22.070 --> 00:44:24.469
Yes, I'm not the CEO,

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:28.859
I'm the President, but I will take the suggestion to the board.

00:44:28.860 --> 00:44:34.184
And I agree for what it's worth, no I'm kidding.

00:44:34.320 --> 00:44:38.399
I mean, you definitely do operate in a different

00:44:38.400 --> 00:44:44.399
way at every - at every stage of the company,

00:44:44.400 --> 00:44:47.109
in the early days when it's just two of you,

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:51.304
we actually spent the - we spent the very early days in Stripe,

00:44:51.450 --> 00:44:55.369
the first few weeks working on it from Buenos Aires,

00:44:55.450 --> 00:44:59.509
it was January break in college and

00:44:59.510 --> 00:45:01.109
we didn't want to work in the U.S,

00:45:01.110 --> 00:45:04.459
because we didn't have - we had student visas and not work visas,

00:45:04.550 --> 00:45:06.029
we didn't want to go home to Ireland,

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:08.219
because it'd be super distracting or whatever.

00:45:08.220 --> 00:45:12.809
So we did the logical thing and we went to Buenos Aires and it was

00:45:12.810 --> 00:45:15.499
awesome that is like a city that works on a hacker schedule,

00:45:15.500 --> 00:45:18.654
everything is open till 5 AM and has Wi-Fi and it's great.

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:24.859
That's a slightly different experience to a 200 person company now.

00:45:25.090 --> 00:45:31.939
And I think what you - you need to not get - you hear these stories of

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:36.599
people who want to stay coding to the detriment of other things,

00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:39.849
at a certain point you have to acknowledge that

00:45:39.850 --> 00:45:43.129
the productive thing for me is not going to be

00:45:43.130 --> 00:45:46.049
directly doing this myself or directly coding,

00:45:46.190 --> 00:45:51.019
but working on something different.

00:45:51.200 --> 00:45:53.889
And you have to get out ahead of that.

00:45:53.890 --> 00:45:56.159
I think the other thing that you have to get good

00:45:56.160 --> 00:45:58.939
at is pretty quickly learning new skills,

00:45:59.010 --> 00:46:02.919
because you're going to be doing things like recruiting or running

00:46:02.920 --> 00:46:05.869
a board meeting or running a staff meeting or running an all-hands

00:46:05.870 --> 00:46:08.719
or all this kind of stuff that you probably haven't done before

00:46:08.820 --> 00:46:15.009
and I think it takes a - it requires you to be willing to be not

00:46:15.010 --> 00:46:18.609
good at things in front of a large group of people and be pretty

00:46:18.610 --> 00:46:21.744
open to feedback to actually get good at those things.

00:46:21.920 --> 00:46:24.339
So on that note, when you look back,

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:27.809
imagine you're starting all over again, it's a few years ago,

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:29.559
because it's really only a few years,

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:33.109
are there any very specific things that you'd go wow,

00:46:33.110 --> 00:46:35.279
I wish I had really done something differently

00:46:35.280 --> 00:46:37.039
that would have been really meaningful?

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:40.104
I mean, help us understand so that everyone here can learn from that?

00:46:40.260 --> 00:46:40.794
Yes.

00:46:41.180 --> 00:46:47.589
I think the reassuring thing and one pattern in lot of the - if

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:51.249
you read these histories of companies is just how much stuff they

00:46:51.250 --> 00:46:54.169
screwed up and that should be a really heartening lesson,

00:46:54.170 --> 00:46:58.429
it's - you can still screw up that much stuff and get away with it.

00:46:58.430 --> 00:47:02.489
There is this - they talk about in the early days of Google

00:47:02.490 --> 00:47:07.639
how they had this one machine I thing it was the script that

00:47:07.640 --> 00:47:11.174
Sergey wrote doing all the indexing of the entire web and

00:47:11.280 --> 00:47:13.329
at a certain point they just like couldn't keep up with the

00:47:13.330 --> 00:47:15.799
growth of the web and everything was slowing down,

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:21.139
search results took multiple seconds to actually respond and

00:47:21.140 --> 00:47:26.254
this was when Google was getting reasonably large and they

00:47:26.390 --> 00:47:30.809
fixed it and it's now - they are now hyper focused on speed,

00:47:30.810 --> 00:47:35.469
but it really was quite broken there along the way and the same

00:47:35.470 --> 00:47:38.549
with Facebook who stumbled through lots of different revenue models

00:47:38.550 --> 00:47:40.539
before arriving at the one they wanted and things like this.

00:47:40.640 --> 00:47:44.604
And so, the reassuring thing is that you can screw up a lot of stuff.

00:47:44.840 --> 00:47:47.399
I think the ... But you didn't right?

00:47:47.400 --> 00:47:51.029
No, no, no, we were - luckily we got everything right.

00:47:51.030 --> 00:47:57.839
I think the two things you really can't screw up are product

00:47:57.840 --> 00:48:02.199
direction where I mean if you go down the wrong road,

00:48:02.200 --> 00:48:08.649
that's really hard to pull back from and the other thing is hiring,

00:48:08.700 --> 00:48:12.399
just because a bad mistake when it

00:48:12.400 --> 00:48:16.079
comes to hiring is so demotivating,

00:48:16.080 --> 00:48:18.694
it can spread so badly to the rest of the company

00:48:18.810 --> 00:48:21.259
that you really have to get a lot of hires right.

00:48:21.260 --> 00:48:23.239
I think the other thing is as you grow,

00:48:23.240 --> 00:48:26.819
the correct way to view hiring is not I'm filling

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:32.989
someone to do this role and fill this slot.

00:48:33.080 --> 00:48:36.419
It's - the correct way to view hiring is branches of a tree that

00:48:36.420 --> 00:48:39.199
when you hire this person you're not only bringing them,

00:48:39.200 --> 00:48:41.599
but you're bringing their effect on the culture and all

00:48:41.600 --> 00:48:45.089
the other people they're going to bring in with them and

00:48:45.090 --> 00:48:50.459
the norms and the working style they have that will spread

00:48:50.460 --> 00:48:54.014
throughout the company and as time goes on,

00:48:54.080 --> 00:48:59.789
those hires have - you have less and less influence on the company

00:48:59.790 --> 00:49:02.619
and all the new people you're bringing in have more,

00:49:02.620 --> 00:49:06.579
and so hiring you can't really screw up.

00:49:07.650 --> 00:49:07.904
Great.

00:49:07.980 --> 00:49:08.569
Yes.

00:49:11.360 --> 00:49:14.079
Can you talk a little bit about how you're going

00:49:14.080 --> 00:49:16.519
to scale that kind of hiring you're doing now,

00:49:16.520 --> 00:49:19.879
so you and Patrick still interview everyone sometimes,

00:49:19.930 --> 00:49:22.364
if people go through projects before you decided to work with them.

00:49:22.510 --> 00:49:25.559
How do you do that as the company goes from 200 to 500 to 1000?

00:49:25.560 --> 00:49:29.889
So the question is how do you scale the hiring process

00:49:29.890 --> 00:49:32.889
to know you've got the right person as you get so big?

00:49:33.620 --> 00:49:35.949
That's a very relevant question.

00:49:35.950 --> 00:49:40.944
It's a discussion we are having internally right now.

00:49:41.280 --> 00:49:50.919
I think a lot of it is as you go from - speaking

00:49:50.920 --> 00:49:54.049
of these questions of scaling,

00:49:54.050 --> 00:49:56.289
let's be clear, Stripe is 200 people right now.

00:49:56.290 --> 00:49:58.539
We are in the process of growing a lot,

00:49:58.540 --> 00:50:01.779
we will probably grow - we'll add a significant

00:50:01.780 --> 00:50:03.214
number of people this year.

00:50:03.300 --> 00:50:04.979
But if we've messed it all up,

00:50:04.980 --> 00:50:06.929
you won't see the results until a few years down the road and

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:10.259
so to take all my advice on scaling with a pinch of salt,

00:50:11.130 --> 00:50:14.789
but with scaling in particular,

00:50:14.790 --> 00:50:19.939
I think it's moving from things being at a gut level,

00:50:19.940 --> 00:50:22.294
this doesn't feel right, I'm not so sure about this,

00:50:22.410 --> 00:50:26.609
to making things very explicit.

00:50:26.610 --> 00:50:28.579
And so, in the early days,

00:50:28.580 --> 00:50:33.019
I think companies spend a lot of time talking about culture fit and

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:38.099
hopefully the various people at the company know what that means and

00:50:38.100 --> 00:50:41.204
know what set of traits that they believe are important in hires,

00:50:41.330 --> 00:50:45.559
but as the company grows you have to make that explicit

00:50:45.560 --> 00:50:49.069
and you have to say this is the set of things we

00:50:49.070 --> 00:50:50.939
believe makes someone succeed at Stripe,

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:52.679
this is the set of traits we believe are important,

00:50:52.680 --> 00:50:55.659
this is the set of skills we think someone needs to come in that

00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:59.999
role and that feels kind of weirdly artificial in the early days

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:03.929
and it's inefficient in the early days because if you're being

00:51:03.930 --> 00:51:06.119
so explicit about everything in a fast moving environment,

00:51:06.120 --> 00:51:07.104
you will get weighed down.

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:10.099
But as the company grows,

00:51:10.100 --> 00:51:12.134
it will end up in this complete game of telephone

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:14.599
unless you're really explicit about with something

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:16.919
like hiring - what is important in a hire?

00:51:17.460 --> 00:51:20.254
So clearly every single person contributes to the culture,

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:22.959
and that's - but that's one of the variables that affects culture.

00:51:22.960 --> 00:51:28.359
Do you think about the culture of the company and what other things

00:51:28.360 --> 00:51:33.049
do - levers do you pull to influence the culture at the organization?

00:51:34.070 --> 00:51:34.394
Yes.

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:38.674
So everyone does and I think,

00:51:38.870 --> 00:51:45.189
some people pitch that as a thing to be

00:51:45.410 --> 00:51:48.339
kept in check or restrained or whatever,

00:51:48.490 --> 00:51:52.509
people often ask the question like how do you keep the culture

00:51:52.510 --> 00:51:56.919
from changing and I guess to me that question suggests a huge

00:51:56.920 --> 00:52:00.899
amount of hubris in that to suggest that initial kernel of

00:52:00.900 --> 00:52:04.429
culture that you had is the perfect culture that any company

00:52:04.430 --> 00:52:07.939
could have and does not deserve any improving,

00:52:07.940 --> 00:52:09.794
yes, something seems off there.

00:52:09.970 --> 00:52:12.524
And so, the correct question is not how

00:52:12.600 --> 00:52:14.444
do you keep the culture from changing?

00:52:14.560 --> 00:52:19.389
It's what culture do you want to have and how do you get there?

00:52:19.480 --> 00:52:21.999
And sometimes you will believe that you have that culture

00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:28.724
already and you want to - you want to teach new hires that.

00:52:28.920 --> 00:52:31.419
But I think often times it will - if you are being really

00:52:31.420 --> 00:52:34.059
thoughtful about it is about recognizing a deficiency

00:52:34.060 --> 00:52:36.359
in your culture and how do we inculcate that.

00:52:36.360 --> 00:52:40.109
And I think I can identify various points throughout the history

00:52:40.110 --> 00:52:44.469
of Stripe where the culture actually improved from someone coming

00:52:44.470 --> 00:52:47.259
in and them bringing in some new culture with them,

00:52:47.260 --> 00:52:49.019
as an example of this,

00:52:49.130 --> 00:52:54.379
I think in the early days start-ups tend to be very fly by night,

00:52:54.380 --> 00:52:58.009
shoot from the hip, not at all metrics driven and maybe that's

00:52:58.010 --> 00:53:02.029
necessary in that maybe you need to have a clear idea of where

00:53:02.030 --> 00:53:04.699
you're going and ignore the data to some degree.

00:53:04.770 --> 00:53:07.149
But it's really important as they grow to become

00:53:07.150 --> 00:53:09.369
more metrics-oriented and actually look at usage,

00:53:09.450 --> 00:53:11.509
look at how people are using the products and

00:53:11.510 --> 00:53:14.799
bake that into future product development.

00:53:14.870 --> 00:53:17.604
And we've definitely started off,

00:53:17.770 --> 00:53:19.729
not very metrics driven and we've become

00:53:19.730 --> 00:53:21.509
much more metrics driven over time.

00:53:21.630 --> 00:53:25.434
And I think that that was a good example of a concrete

00:53:25.520 --> 00:53:28.919
place I can identify the culture as having improved.

00:53:29.270 --> 00:53:31.659
There are other factors that always come

00:53:31.660 --> 00:53:35.729
into play like the physical space, different rewards, incentives,

00:53:35.730 --> 00:53:39.609
do you think about that as part of the culture as well?

00:53:39.610 --> 00:53:47.659
It is though, it's a bit more I think the - hopefully the physical

00:53:47.660 --> 00:53:53.029
space is a manifestation of something different - sorry a manifestation

00:53:53.030 --> 00:53:59.289
of something larger rather than the culture itself and that,

00:53:59.290 --> 00:54:03.329
if your culture is we have foosball,

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:05.844
it's a bit like - cool.

00:54:06.080 --> 00:54:07.419
Okay, great.

00:54:07.420 --> 00:54:11.914
But if your culture is - at Stripe for example,

00:54:12.000 --> 00:54:15.719
we serve meals at the company and rather than having

00:54:15.720 --> 00:54:17.649
cafe style seating with the round table,

00:54:17.650 --> 00:54:20.539
there will be long dining room tables kind of Hogwart's

00:54:20.540 --> 00:54:24.359
style and we think that's really valuable because

00:54:24.360 --> 00:54:26.979
it means that in a fast growing team,

00:54:26.980 --> 00:54:31.069
people get the chance to meet each other that they don't - that they might

00:54:31.070 --> 00:54:34.859
not otherwise and we very much don't want Stripe to become these silos

00:54:34.860 --> 00:54:37.499
where you have the finance people and the marketing people,

00:54:37.500 --> 00:54:39.609
and the engineers and they're all in their kind of cliques.

00:54:39.610 --> 00:54:42.599
We want it to be the case that people at Stripe have a lot

00:54:42.600 --> 00:54:45.119
of empathy for how different parts of the company work and

00:54:45.120 --> 00:54:48.279
are pretty well rounded and so that's an example I point

00:54:48.280 --> 00:54:52.549
at as where the space is hopefully an indication of some

00:54:52.550 --> 00:54:54.999
larger cultural thing rather than a foosball table.

00:54:55.150 --> 00:54:55.524
Great.

00:54:55.760 --> 00:54:56.629
That's exactly what I meant.

00:54:56.700 --> 00:54:57.369
Great.

00:54:57.760 --> 00:54:57.889
Yes.

00:54:57.890 --> 00:55:01.009
Please stand up and speak really loud.

00:55:01.920 --> 00:55:06.949
What would be your advice for working or not with a person or a team?

00:55:09.071 --> 00:55:10.287
Can you repeat it?

00:55:10.386 --> 00:55:10.944
Yes.

00:55:11.086 --> 00:55:16.329
What would be your advice for working or not with a person or a team?

00:55:16.330 --> 00:55:17.739
What would be your advice for working or

00:55:17.740 --> 00:55:20.249
not working with a person or a team?

00:55:20.250 --> 00:55:26.117
You mean as a co-founder or when it comes to hiring?

00:55:26.217 --> 00:55:26.982
Both.

00:55:27.082 --> 00:55:27.415
Okay.

00:55:33.210 --> 00:55:39.959
I guess the two things to think about are - one,

00:55:39.960 --> 00:55:44.719
I think the quality and tenor of your personal

00:55:44.720 --> 00:55:46.744
interactions actually matters a lot.

00:55:46.820 --> 00:55:50.879
There are some things that are absolutely table stakes like

00:55:50.880 --> 00:55:53.369
honesty and integrity and if you see any bad signs there,

00:55:53.370 --> 00:55:57.229
you should run away at 100 miles an hour.

00:55:57.230 --> 00:56:02.349
But then there is deeper things about your interaction,

00:56:02.480 --> 00:56:04.259
and your working style,

00:56:04.260 --> 00:56:07.694
I think one interesting question that you should really think about is

00:56:07.910 --> 00:56:12.444
how much should you argue with someone you're working closely with,

00:56:12.580 --> 00:56:18.499
because it's not obvious that you want to be in - you clearly just don't

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:21.429
want to be the two of the same people that's kind of wasteful,

00:56:21.430 --> 00:56:27.224
you hopefully have some conflict and some diversity of opinion,

00:56:27.400 --> 00:56:29.919
such that you're both bringing different perspectives to the table,

00:56:29.920 --> 00:56:33.749
but at the same time, you probably don't want to be too far,

00:56:33.750 --> 00:56:37.444
too diverse in opinions where Max Levchin

00:56:37.530 --> 00:56:40.359
describes how when they merged x.com

00:56:40.360 --> 00:56:47.019
and PayPal, PayPal were all Unix people and x.com

00:56:47.020 --> 00:56:50.879
were all Windows Server people and they kind of smushed them together

00:56:50.880 --> 00:56:54.799
and it was bedlam because you had these two people who had - red team,

00:56:54.800 --> 00:56:57.909
blue team, two completely different ways of doing things and it's

00:56:58.010 --> 00:57:02.899
unclear whether one was better than the other in that context,

00:57:02.900 --> 00:57:04.314
but they certainly didn't work together.

00:57:04.420 --> 00:57:10.099
And so if it's productive disagreement that's - that let you know,

00:57:10.100 --> 00:57:12.384
that results in a better product then that's great.

00:57:12.450 --> 00:57:16.039
If it's kind of stylistic disagreement that you can't get past and

00:57:16.040 --> 00:57:18.789
you do just have two different approaches for doing something,

00:57:18.890 --> 00:57:21.209
then that's maybe a sign that you're not going

00:57:21.210 --> 00:57:22.689
to be able to work that well together.

00:57:23.480 --> 00:57:26.119
I'm sure that you would agree this was absolutely fascinating.

00:57:26.120 --> 00:57:28.090
Please join me in thanking John Collison.

